Digital Health Check Rubric  ·  Weston College Group  ·  2025–26

**Digital Health Check**

Accessibility & Inclusion Rubric — Microsoft Forms Setup Guide

Weston College Group · Digital Pedagogy Coach · 2025–26

**About this document**

This rubric defines the five dimensions of the Digital Health Check used to assess accessibility and inclusive digital practice across Weston College curriculum areas. Each dimension has five indicators, each scored on a 1–5 Likert scale: 1 (Urgent) through to 5 (Confident). The rubric is used during developmental observations, learning walks, and area health check visits.

Part 1 contains the full rubric for each dimension — intended as your reference during assessments. Part 2 contains the Microsoft Forms setup guide, including the Copilot prompt you can paste directly into Forms to generate the 25-question Likert scale form automatically.

**How to score**

5 — Confident: Exemplary, embedded, could model for others.

4 — On Track: Consistent and effective with minor gaps.

3 — Developing: Some evidence, not yet consistent or systematic.

2 — Challenged: Awareness present but little or no practice.

1 — Urgent: Not present. Significant barrier to inclusive practice.

N/O — Not Observable: Leave blank. Only scored indicators count in dimension average.

**Part 1 — Full Rubric**

◬  **Accessibility by Design**

*How to observe: Mostly observable via resources shared in Teams, on screen, or in lesson materials.*

| **Indicator** | **How to observe** | **1 — Urgent** | **2 — Challenged** | **3 — Developing** | **4 — On Track** | **5 — Confident** |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| **Uses Accessibility Checker** | *Check if files in Teams have been run through Checker (green tick in Word/PowerPoint). Ask to demonstrate.* | No awareness of the Accessibility Checker. Has never used it. Resources shared without any checking. | Aware the tool exists but has never used it or used it only once. No habit formed. | Uses the Accessibility Checker occasionally but not as a routine before sharing. Some resources pass; others don't. | Runs Accessibility Checker before sharing most resources. Minor gaps remain (e.g. older files not updated). | Accessibility Checker is automatic — every resource checked before sharing. Issues fixed before distribution. Could demonstrate to a peer. |
| **Alt text on images** | *Inspect images in shared PowerPoints or Word docs. Right-click → Edit Alt Text.* | No alt text on any images. No awareness that alt text is needed or why. | Knows alt text exists but rarely applies it. Most images have no description. | Adds alt text to some images — typically those just created, not retrospectively. Quality varies. | Alt text present on most images. Some older resources not updated. Descriptions are functional if basic. | All images have meaningful, descriptive alt text. Marks decorative images as decorative. Can explain why to learners. |
| **Colour contrast checked** | *Inspect slide decks and documents. Look for light-on-light or dark-on-dark combinations.* | No awareness of colour contrast as an accessibility issue. Uses colours chosen purely for aesthetics. | Aware contrast matters but applies no systematic check. May use problematic combinations unknowingly. | Takes some care with colour choices but doesn't verify against WCAG 2.2 AA standards. Some slides/docs still fail. | Consciously checks that text is readable against backgrounds. Uses high-contrast defaults in most materials. | All materials meet WCAG 2.2 AA contrast (4.5:1 for normal text). Uses contrast-checking tools. Never relies on colour alone to convey meaning. |
| **Captions/transcripts on video** | *Play any video shared in Teams or embedded in resources. Check captions are enabled/provided.* | Videos shared with no captions or transcripts. No awareness this is required. | Aware captions should exist but relies on auto-captions only, without reviewing accuracy. | Enables auto-captions on videos. Does not create or edit transcripts. Some videos still shared without. | Auto-captions enabled consistently. Reviews them for accuracy on key videos. Transcripts provided for important content. | All video content captioned. Captions reviewed and corrected where needed. Transcripts provided. Learners explicitly told how to use them. |
| **Accessible assignment briefs** | *Inspect assignment briefs in Teams Assignments or shared folders. Check headings, font, structure.* | Briefs shared as images, scanned PDFs, or dense unformatted text. No headings, no structure. Inaccessible by design. | Briefs are typed but formatting is ad-hoc. No consistent heading structure. Font choice or size may cause issues. | Some formatting applied (bold headings, readable font) but not systematic. Not checked with Accessibility Checker. | Briefs use clear headings, readable font (12pt+), good spacing. Passes Accessibility Checker. Minor improvements possible. | Briefs are fully accessible: proper heading hierarchy, readable font and size, sufficient spacing, passes checker, available in alternative formats on request. |

◎  **Promoting Accessible Practice**

*How to observe: Mostly observable during lesson delivery or from learner testimony. Some aspects require direct observation.*

| **Indicator** | **How to observe** | **1 — Urgent** | **2 — Challenged** | **3 — Developing** | **4 — On Track** | **5 — Confident** |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| **Proactively offers Immersive Reader** | *Watch whether teacher mentions Immersive Reader during lesson or in Teams resources. Can also ask learners.* | Has never mentioned Immersive Reader to learners. Unaware it exists or how to activate it. | Aware of Immersive Reader but has not demonstrated it to learners. May mention it if directly asked. | Has shown Immersive Reader to learners at least once but does not offer it routinely. Relies on learners to self-identify need. | Mentions Immersive Reader regularly. Demonstrates it at start of term. Most learners know it exists. | Proactively offers Immersive Reader to all learners as a standard tool. Demonstrates it in every new topic or resource. Learners use it independently. |
| **Mentions AT tools in lessons** | *Watch for verbal or visual references to Read**&**Write, Live Captions, Dictate, Focus Mode during lesson.* | No mention of any AT tools in lesson. Assistive technology is invisible in teaching practice. | AT tools mentioned only when a specific learner flags a need. No proactive normalisation. | Mentions one or two AT tools occasionally. Not embedded into lesson structure or delivery. | Refers to AT tools (Immersive Reader, Live Captions, Read&Write) in most lessons. Knows the key tools available. | AT tools woven into every lesson naturally. Mentions Read&Write, Dictate, Live Captions, Focus Mode as standard options. Learners reminded regularly. |
| **Accessibility offered universally — not SEND-only** | *Does the teacher offer tools to all learners or only direct SEND learners? Ask learners.* | Accessibility tools only discussed with learners who have a formal SEND diagnosis. Other learners never offered them. | Mainly directed at SEND learners. Occasionally mentions to others but not as a universal offer. | Becoming more universal but still defaults to SEND-focused framing (e.g. "if you have dyslexia..."). | Frames accessibility tools as useful for everyone. Offers them broadly. Occasional slip back to SEND-only language. | Explicitly positions accessibility as universal ("these tools help everyone"). No SEND-only framing. Normalised for whole class. |
| **Learners personalise their digital environment** | *Can learners be seen adjusting display settings, using preferred tools, or navigating independently?* | Learners have no awareness that their digital environment can be personalised. No personalisation visible. | Some learners may have set preferences but this has not been taught or encouraged by the teacher. | Teacher has mentioned personalisation options once or twice. A few learners use them but most don't know how. | Most learners aware of basic personalisation (e.g. dark mode, text size, Immersive Reader settings). Some use them. | Learners confidently personalise their environment. Teacher has taught them how. Diverse personalisation visible across the class. |
| **AT embedded into routine delivery** | *Is AT referenced in lesson plans, slide decks, assignment briefs, Teams resources, or verbal delivery?* | AT is entirely absent from lesson design and delivery. Not referenced anywhere. | AT appears occasionally but only as a reactive response to a learner difficulty, not planned. | AT is planned for some lessons but not consistently. Referenced in some resources but not built into delivery. | AT is part of most lesson plans. Teacher consistently references it. Embedded in Teams resources and delivery. | AT is fully embedded: referenced in lesson plans, Teams channels, assignment briefs, and verbal delivery. Learners expect it as standard. |

◈  **Inclusive Knowledge ****&**** Practice**

*How to observe: Mix of observable (lesson design, resource variety) and inference from conversation and learner outcomes.*

| **Indicator** | **How to observe** | **1 — Urgent** | **2 — Challenged** | **3 — Developing** | **4 — On Track** | **5 — Confident** |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| **Aware of SEND needs and adjusts digitally** | *Look for differentiated digital resources, personalised Teams setups, or ask about specific adjustments made.* | No awareness of how SEND needs connect to digital practice. Adjustments not considered in resource design. | Aware learners have needs but digital adjustments are not made. Refers learners to SEND team without digital follow-through. | Makes some digital adjustments for known SEND learners (e.g. larger font, different colour background) but not systematically. | Proactively considers SEND needs when designing digital resources. Most learners with identified needs have adjustments in place. | Fully informed about cohort SEND needs. Digital adjustments made proactively for all. Reviews and updates as needs change. Works with SEND team. |
| **Applies UDL — multiple means of engagement** | *Look for variety in how content is presented (text, audio, video, visual) and how learners can respond.* | Single delivery format throughout. All learners receive the same resource in the same way. No variation. | Occasionally provides a second format (e.g. a video alongside text) but not by design — ad hoc. | Some UDL principles applied — at least two means of representation visible. Not yet consistent across lessons. | Multiple means of representation used consistently. Learners offered some choice in how they engage with content. | UDL embedded across all lessons. Multiple means of representation, expression, and engagement. Learner choice visible and genuinely offered. |
| **Provides digital alternatives or choices** | *Do learners have a genuine choice of how to submit or engage? Are alternatives offered in assignment briefs?* | One submission format, one way to engage. No alternatives offered digitally. | Occasionally allows a different format if a learner requests it. Not built into the design. | Some tasks offer a digital alternative (e.g. typed or spoken response). Not consistent across all tasks. | Most tasks offer at least two ways to engage or submit. Alternatives are signposted clearly. | Digital alternatives are designed in from the start. Learners routinely choose how to engage and submit. Choices are genuinely equivalent. |
| **Resources designed for equitable access** | *Inspect resources. Are font sizes readable, layouts clean, images explained, language plain and clear?* | Resources are inaccessible by default — dense text, jargon-heavy, no visual support, no plain language consideration. | Some basic formatting present but resources not designed with equity in mind. Significant barriers remain. | Resources are generally readable but not systematically designed for equitable access. Some barriers present. | Most resources designed with equitable access in mind: plain language, clear layout, alt text, accessible fonts. | All resources designed for equitable access from the outset. Plain language, visual support, multiple formats, and accessibility features built in by default. |
| **Understands digital inclusion vs. digital skills** | *Gauge through conversation: can the teacher distinguish between learner access barriers and skill gaps?* | No distinction made. Access barriers treated the same as skill gaps. Inclusion not considered separately. | Some awareness that inclusion matters but conflates it with skills training. No distinct strategies. | Understands the difference conceptually but does not yet apply different strategies to each. | Can articulate the distinction and applies different responses to access barriers vs. skill gaps. | Clear and practised understanding. Actively addresses inclusion barriers (device, connectivity, language, disability) separately from skills development. |

⊡  **Digital Organisation ****&**** Hygiene**

*How to observe: Largely observable — look at Teams Files, SharePoint, shared folder structures, and file naming conventions.*

| **Indicator** | **How to observe** | **1 — Urgent** | **2 — Challenged** | **3 — Developing** | **4 — On Track** | **5 — Confident** |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| **Teams/SharePoint folder structure is logical** | *Ask to see Teams channels or SharePoint folders. Look at hierarchy, naming, and findability.* | No folder structure. Files dumped in one location. Impossible to navigate without knowing exactly what you're looking for. | Some folders exist but named vaguely or inconsistently. Structure not logical. Learners struggle to find resources. | Basic folder structure in place. Main resources findable. Subfolders inconsistent or missing. Needs tidying. | Clear folder structure: module/topic/week organised logically. Labels descriptive. Learners can navigate independently. | Well-structured folder hierarchy that mirrors the curriculum. Named consistently. Reviewed regularly. Learners and colleagues can find anything quickly. |
| **GDPR-compliant sharing** | *Look for personal data (learner names, grades, health info) in shared or open files.* | Personal learner data (names, grades, health/SEND info) shared in open files or links. Significant GDPR risk. | Some awareness of GDPR but personal data occasionally included in shared resources without appropriate controls. | Generally avoids putting personal data in shared resources but not always consistent. Unsure about edge cases. | Personal data handled appropriately. Not included in open-access resources. Shares only aggregated or anonymised data. | Fully GDPR-compliant digital practice. Personal data kept in secure, access-controlled locations only. Could advise colleagues on what is and isn't acceptable. |
| **Version control — no ****"****final_v3****"**** files** | *Check file names in Teams and shared folders for evidence of version sprawl.* | Multiple versions of the same file with confusing names (final, final2, USE THIS ONE). No clear current version. | Some version confusion present but not severe. Occasionally can't tell which version is current. | Mostly maintains one working version but occasionally saves duplicates. No formal version control. | Consistent use of versioning in file names (e.g. v1, v2) or relies on OneDrive version history. Current version clear. | Uses OneDrive/SharePoint version history or clear naming conventions. No redundant files. Archives old versions in a separate folder. Could teach this to others. |
| **Naming conventions are consistent** | *Review file and folder names across Teams and SharePoint for consistency and clarity.* | Inconsistent, cryptic, or meaningless file names throughout. Cannot determine content without opening the file. | Some descriptive naming but no consistent convention. File names vary in format across folders. | Mostly descriptive names. Some inconsistency. Colleagues could find most things but not reliably. | Consistent naming pattern applied across most files and folders. Clear, descriptive, includes date or version where appropriate. | Exemplary naming conventions applied consistently. Files immediately identifiable. Could serve as a model for others. |
| **Digital resources reviewed and updated regularly** | *Check file modification dates and ask when resources were last reviewed.* | Resources unchanged from initial creation. No review process. Some materials are outdated or incorrect. | Resources updated when errors are noticed, not proactively. Review is reactive. | Some resources reviewed and updated termly. Others left unchanged. No systematic review process. | Most resources reviewed at least annually. Updates made before each delivery. Outdated materials archived or deleted. | Systematic review cycle in place. All resources reviewed before delivery. Outdated materials removed. Improvements tracked. |

◷  **Effective Digital Communication**

*How to observe: Observable — review Teams channels, posts, announcements, and assignment feedback visible during visit.*

| **Indicator** | **How to observe** | **1 — Urgent** | **2 — Challenged** | **3 — Developing** | **4 — On Track** | **5 — Confident** |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| **Teams channel/class space is organised** | *Open Teams and view the class space. Look at channel structure, pinned resources, and General channel posts.* | Teams space either doesn't exist, is entirely empty, or is so cluttered it's unusable. Learners have no functional digital class space. | Teams space exists but minimal setup. Resources missing, channels not organised, learners unlikely to use it. | Teams space is functional. Basic resources present but not curated. Some clutter. Navigation possible but effortful. | Well-organised Teams space. Relevant resources pinned or in Files. Posts are purposeful. Learners directed there regularly. | Teams space is a curated digital environment. Clear channels, pinned resources up to date, organised Files tab, consistent communication. Learners use it as their primary learning hub. |
| **Posts and messages are clear and professional** | *Read recent posts in Teams General channel and any announcements. Check tone, clarity, and professionalism.* | No posts or messages. Or posts are unclear, informal to the point of confusion, or contain errors that undermine confidence. | Posts exist but are irregular and sometimes unclear. Tone may be too informal or messages lack key information. | Posts are generally professional and clear. Occasional inconsistency in tone or detail. Learners mostly understand what is expected. | Posts and messages consistently professional, clear, and timely. Learners know what they need to do. Rare confusion. | Exemplary digital communication. Clear, professional, warm tone. Posts anticipate learner questions. Messages include everything learners need. Could be used as a model. |
| **Appropriate Teams features used** | *Look for use of Assignments (not just General posts), announcements, tags, tabs, and relevant channels.* | Only uses General channel. No Assignments, no tabs, no structured features. Teams used like a basic messaging app. | Uses Assignments occasionally but not consistently. Other features (tabs, announcements, channels) largely unused. | Uses Assignments regularly. Some additional features used (e.g. a Resources tab). Not yet using the full potential. | Uses Assignments, appropriate channels, and tabs effectively. Announcements used for key information. Good feature awareness. | Full and purposeful use of Teams features: Assignments with rubrics, relevant channels, tabs, tags, announcements. Each feature used for a clear pedagogical reason. |
| **Resources proactively shared** | *Check Teams Files tab and recent posts. Are resources uploaded before or alongside lessons, not after?* | Resources not shared digitally. Learners have no way to access materials outside the lesson. | Resources occasionally uploaded to Teams but inconsistently and often after the lesson. | Most lesson resources shared on Teams. Some gaps. Learners mostly know where to find them. | Resources shared in advance of or alongside lessons consistently. Learners can prepare and revisit independently. | Resources proactively shared with clear organisation and signposting. Learners directed to specific materials. Includes supplementary resources for further learning. |
| **Communication is accessible and inclusive** | *Check if posts use plain language, include alt text on images, and are formatted for readability.* | Communication assumes all learners can read and engage with standard text without support. No accessibility consideration. | Accessible communication considered occasionally but not consistently. Most posts are plain text with no formatting support. | Some accessible formatting used in posts (bold key info, short paragraphs). Not systematic. No alt text on images in posts. | Posts formatted for readability. Plain language used. Key information highlighted. Some consideration for learners with English as additional language. | All communication is accessible: plain language, clear formatting, alt text on images in posts, inclusive of ESOL and SEND learners. Tone is warm and welcoming for all. |

**Part 2 — Microsoft Forms Setup Guide**

**Overview**

The Digital Health Check Form uses a Likert scale (1–5) for each of the 25 indicators across five dimensions. The form is designed to be completed by Graeme Wright (or a Digital Lead) during or immediately after a health check visit. Copilot in Microsoft Forms can generate the full question set from the prompt below.

**Setup steps**

1. Go to forms.office.com and sign in with your Weston College Microsoft account.

2. Click + New Form.

3. Give the form a title: Digital Health Check — [Area Name] — [Date]

4. Click the Copilot button (sparkle icon, top right of the form editor).

5. Paste the prompt from the next section into the Copilot input field.

6. Review the generated questions against Part 1 of this document.

7. Add the following header fields manually before the Likert questions:

   — Short answer: Curriculum area

   — Short answer: Teacher/Assessor name

   — Date: Visit date

   — Short answer: Observer name (pre-fill: Graeme Wright)

   — Long answer: Context / notes

8. Add a final Long answer question: Overall observations and recommended next steps

9. Set all Likert questions to Required: No (some indicators may not be observable on every visit).

10. Share via the Forms link — or use Collect responses → Copy link to send directly.

**Copilot Prompt**

Copy the entire block below and paste it into the Copilot input in Microsoft Forms. Do not edit the prompt before pasting — Copilot will generate the questions directly from it.

Create a Microsoft Form for a Digital Health Check assessment at Weston College.

 

The form should have 25 Likert scale questions grouped into 5 sections. Each question uses a

5-point scale with these exact labels: 1 = Urgent, 2 = Challenged, 3 = Developing, 4 = On Track, 5 = Confident.

 

Set all questions as optional (not required), so the assessor can skip indicators not observable on a given visit.

 

Section 1: Accessibility by Design (5 questions)

1. Uses Accessibility Checker — runs Accessibility Checker before sharing resources in Teams

2. Alt text on images — adds meaningful alt text to images in shared documents and slides

3. Colour contrast checked — verifies text/background contrast meets WCAG 2.2 AA standards

4. Captions/transcripts on video — provides captions or transcripts for all shared video content

5. Accessible assignment briefs — assignment briefs use clear headings, readable fonts, and accessible structure

 

Section 2: Promoting Accessible Practice (5 questions)

6. Proactively offers Immersive Reader — demonstrates and encourages Immersive Reader use for all learners

7. Mentions AT tools in lessons — references assistive technology tools (Read&Write, Dictate, Live Captions) in lessons

8. Accessibility offered universally — frames accessibility tools as useful for all learners, not SEND-only

9. Learners personalise their digital environment — teaches and encourages learners to adjust their digital settings

10. AT embedded into routine delivery — assistive technology is planned into lesson design, not added reactively

 

Section 3: Inclusive Knowledge and Practice (5 questions)

11. Aware of SEND needs and adjusts digitally — makes digital adjustments based on cohort SEND needs

12. Applies UDL — multiple means of engagement — provides multiple formats for content representation and learner response

13. Provides digital alternatives or choices — offers genuine choice in how learners engage with and submit work

14. Resources designed for equitable access — resources use plain language, clear layout, and accessible design

15. Understands digital inclusion vs digital skills — can distinguish access barriers from skill gaps and respond differently

 

Section 4: Digital Organisation and Hygiene (5 questions)

16. Teams/SharePoint folder structure is logical — folder hierarchy is clear, labelled, and navigable by learners

17. GDPR-compliant sharing — personal learner data is not shared in open files or links

18. Version control — no "final_v3" files — uses consistent version naming or OneDrive version history

19. Naming conventions are consistent — files and folders use clear, descriptive, consistent naming

20. Digital resources reviewed and updated regularly — resources are reviewed before delivery and outdated materials removed

 

Section 5: Effective Digital Communication (5 questions)

21. Teams channel/class space is organised — Teams space has clear channels, pinned resources, and is actively used

22. Posts and messages are clear and professional — Teams posts are well-written, timely, and easy to understand

23. Appropriate Teams features used — uses Assignments, channels, tabs, and announcements purposefully

24. Resources proactively shared — resources are shared in advance or alongside lessons, not retrospectively

25. Communication is accessible and inclusive — posts use plain language, alt text, and inclusive formatting

 

After all 25 questions, add one final Long answer question:

Overall observations and recommended next steps (open text).

